# Kubernetes Node API profile

Generated by create-croco-app for `--preset production-app --backend-deploy kubernetes`.
The API uses the existing Node Host, HTTP transport (including health-core readiness),
compiled DI and Docker prune/build pipeline. The SPA is built separately and is not deployed by this profile.
Helm and a controller are unnecessary for these three native resources.

This is a minimal **uncertified** profile. Offline schema validation and a loopback Node
Host smoke are separate from live cluster deployment. Neither Docker Compose nor a
successful build proves cluster support. `croco-infrastructure-profile.manifest.json`
records ownership, generator version, runtime/build/configuration and file hashes;
its initial `not-run`/`not-verified` fields are not test results. `pnpm k8s:check`
checks drift only. After reviewing intentional changes, `pnpm k8s:lock` records new
hashes and clears verification claims. Do not copy certification results between clusters.

## Build and validate without a cluster

```sh
pnpm install
pnpm build
pnpm typecheck
pnpm k8s:check
# Use an independently installed kubeconform and the schema version of your target cluster.
kubeconform -strict -summary -kubernetes-version 1.34.0 kubernetes/resources.yaml
# Keep the lockfile; the image's frozen install fails if it is missing or stale.
docker build -f apps/api-server/Dockerfile -t croco-api:local .
```

The runtime command is `node apps/api-server/dist/kubernetes.js`, PID 1 without a shell.
It binds `PORT` (3000 in the ConfigMap) on all interfaces. The Service's named target
and all probes point to the same port. `/health/live` remains live during drain;
`/health/ready` returns 503 immediately when shutdown begins. SIGTERM/SIGINT shares
one close promise: the existing middleware rejects new business requests, drains
active requests and events within 20 seconds, and allows a 5-second endpoint
propagation window within that budget. The existing Node Host then closes with its
30-second deadline, followed by telemetry flush/shutdown and application scope disposal.
The Pod has 120 seconds total; tune it together with exporter/provider cleanup budgets.
A timeout is a failed shutdown, not a guarantee of remote side-effect cancellation.
No public HTTP drain endpoint is exposed.

## Review and deploy to your own cluster

Use a dedicated namespace: resource names are `croco-api`, `croco-api-config` and
`croco-api-secrets`. Build/push the image to your registry, replace `image` with the
reviewed digest, configure `WEB_ORIGIN`, and review requests/limits for your workload.
Run `pnpm k8s:lock`, `pnpm k8s:check`, build/typecheck and schema checks again.

The optional `croco-api-secrets` Secret is created out of band. No real or placeholder
credentials are emitted. Add only provider variables the application actually consumes;
adding `DATABASE_URL` alone does not replace the sample repository. Secret env values
are read at process startup: roll Pods after rotation. The .dockerignore excludes
Git metadata, environment files and key files. Keep all other secret files outside
the build context. ConfigMap/Secret keys must not override the reviewed `PORT`,
`NODE_ENV`, or lifecycle/telemetry configuration unintentionally.

```sh
# Explicitly choose your reviewed context and namespace; these commands mutate a cluster.
kubectl --context YOUR_CONTEXT -n YOUR_NAMESPACE apply --dry-run=server -f kubernetes/resources.yaml
kubectl --context YOUR_CONTEXT -n YOUR_NAMESPACE apply -f kubernetes/resources.yaml
kubectl --context YOUR_CONTEXT -n YOUR_NAMESPACE rollout status deployment/croco-api
kubectl --context YOUR_CONTEXT -n YOUR_NAMESPACE port-forward service/croco-api 8080:80
# In another terminal: curl --fail http://localhost:8080/health/ready
```

For live acceptance, record context/cluster version, image digest, server-side validation,
probe success, Secret/environment wiring (without values), Pod deletion during a controlled
in-flight request, readiness/endpoint removal, completion and exit status. Do not mark a
cluster certified without that evidence. This repository does not supply credentials or
perform that live deployment by default.

## Limits and recovery

The sample user repository and rate limiter are process-local. One replica with Recreate
avoids suggesting shared state or rolling consistency; restarts lose sample data and
updates have downtime. External DB/Redis/Queue adapters and their health indicators must
be explicitly composed and verified before running a stateful service. No database,
queue, ingress, TLS, autoscaler or migration owner is installed. Shared typed provider
binding integration is tracked in #3547; this profile does not claim it is implemented.

Revert the reviewed resource file and image digest to roll back code. This does not
restore lost in-memory data, reverse external effects or undo schema migrations. Inspect
Pod events/logs for startup, missing Secret/provider settings, image pull and admission
failures. A failing readiness check prevents Service routing; investigate its cause rather
than replacing it with a liveness check.

See Kubernetes documentation for [termination](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-lifecycle/#pod-termination),
[Secret environment references](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/#using-secrets-as-environment-variables)
and [security contexts](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/security-context/).
